BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lancasterbirdclub": "https://lancasterbirdclub.org/event/monthly-club-meeting-weidensaul/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lancasterbirdclub
X-SOURCE:Lancaster Bird Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T203000
DTSTAMP:20260915T041751Z
CREATED:20250728T194319Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T041751Z
UID:2248-1791486000-1791491400@lancasterbirdclub.org
SUMMARY:Monthly Club Meeting - “The Return of the Oystercatcher: Saving Birds to Save the Planet" w/ Scott Weidensaul
DESCRIPTION:Club Meeting – North Museum of Nature and Science\, 400 College Ave\, Lancaster\, PA 17603 \nPresented by Scott Weidensaul\, author of “The Return of the Oystercatcher: Saving Birds to Save the Planet” \nAt a time when the news for birds – for the planet – has rarely seemed more grim\, there is reason for hope. We’ve restored faltering bird populations in the past\, bringing back once nearly extinct raptors and waterfowl. And despite the odds today\, all around the world\, people are actually reviving bird populations\, providing a roadmap for wider recovery. In his newest book\, “The Return of the Oystercatcher: Saving Birds to Save the Planet\,” best-selling author and researcher Scott Weidensaul shows why we should never underestimate the resiliency of birds\, and why creating a world that works for birds will work for everything\, including us. This is a dose of optimism we all need. \nScott Weidensaul is the author of nearly 30 books on natural history\, including the Pulitzer Prize finalist “Living on the Wind” and his the New York Times bestseller “A World on the Wing.” His latest book\, “Return of the Oystercatcher\,” showcases what’s working for bird conservation around the world. Weidensaul is a contributing editor for Audubon magazine\, a columnist for BWD\, and writes for a variety of other publications including Living Bird. He is a Fellow of the American Ornithological Society and an active field researcher\, studying saw-whet owl migration for 30 years\, as well as winter hummingbirds in the East\, bird migration in Alaska\, and the winter movements of snowy owls through Project SNOWstorm\, which he co-founded. A native of Pennsylvania\, he now lives in New Hampshire.
URL:https://lancasterbirdclub.org/event/monthly-club-meeting-weidensaul/
LOCATION:North Museum of Nature and Science\, 400 College Ave\, Lancaster\, PA\, 17603\, United States
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Saintjameslancaster": "https://www.saintjameslancaster.org/event/mercy-seminar-term-3/2026-10-08/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:saintjameslancaster
X-SOURCE:Saint James Lancaster
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261008T203000
DTSTAMP:20260826T165152Z
CREATED:20260730T183634Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260826T165152Z
UID:10076534-1791486000-1791491400@www.saintjameslancaster.org
SUMMARY:Mercy Seminar\, Term 3
DESCRIPTION:With Father David Peck\nand Professor Randall Zachman\nEntirely on Zoom \nNow in its seventh year\, the 2026 Mercy Seminar invites you to explore the profound insights that different faith traditions offer about embracing life and navigating death with grace. How can the certainty of death inspire us to cultivate a deeper focus and a heart full of gratitude\, transforming potential feelings of anxiety into a courageous appreciation for the present moment? Is death merely “nothing at all\,” or does it open the gateway to an eternal adventure beyond this life? \nJoin us as we delve into selected sacred readings from Hindu\, Buddhist\, Jewish\, Christian\, and Muslim scriptures\, along with enlightening contemporary secular spiritual writings. Together\, we will uncover the wisdom that encourages us to live our best lives and face death not with fear\, but with hope and understanding. \nIn term three (September 24 – October 29)\, we will focus on Christian understandings of resurrection and eternal life\, beginning with the hope of future resurrection life in Paul’s letters and the expectation of eternal life now in the Gospel of John. We will follow the development of these themes in Christian and Spiritualist traditions\, including Emanuel Swedenborg’s provocative visions of the soul’s journey after death. \nClasses meet on six consecutive Thursday evenings via Zoom from 7:00 to 8:30 PM EST. Each term stands alone\, but when taken together\, offers a layered understanding as our knowledge of the issues increases. All sessions are recorded and available to participants for asynchronous viewing. \n\nEach session will be recorded in Zoom and available for viewing for those who have completed their registration. \nCost: $75/term\, includes access to course readings\, recorded sessions for\nasynchronous viewing\, instructor office hours and podcasts. \nScholarships are available; please email Kate Umble Smucker for more information. \nClick here to register for Term 3.
URL:https://www.saintjameslancaster.org/event/mercy-seminar-term-3/2026-10-08/
LOCATION:Zoom Video Conferencing\, PA\, United States
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://www.saintjameslancaster.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/2026-Mercy-Seminar-Term-1-FEATURED-IMAGE-copy.jpg
GEO:41.2033216;-77.1945247
END:VEVENT
